== Prefatory Address to King Francis

#align(center)[TO]

#align(center)[HIS MOST CHRISTIAN MAJESTY,]

#align(center)[THE MOST MIGHTY AND ILLUSTRIOUS MONARCH,]

#align(center)[FRANCIS, KING OF THE FRENCH,]

#align(center)[HIS SOVEREIGN;#footnote[In Calvin’s last edition the words are simply, as translated here, “Principi suo.” In the edition published at Basle \[Basel\] in 1536 they are “Principi ac Domino suo sibi observando.” (Beveridge’s note.)]]

#align(center)[JOHN CALVIN PRAYS PEACE AND SALVATION IN CHRIST.#footnote[The 1536 edition has “In Domino” (in the Lord). (Beveridge’s note.)]]

#smallcaps[Sire], when I first took up this work, nothing was farther from my thoughts than to write something that would afterwards be presented to Your Majesty. My intention was only to provide a kind of first lessons, by which those who feel some zeal for religion might be trained in true piety. And I labored at the task chiefly for the sake of my countrymen the French. I saw that many of them were hungering and thirsting after Christ, while very few seemed to have been properly given even a slender knowledge of him. That this was the aim I had in view is plain from the work itself, which is written in a simple and elementary form suited to teaching.

But when I saw that the fury of certain wicked men had risen to such a height in your realm that there was no place in it for sound doctrine, I thought it might be useful if in the same work I both gave instruction to my countrymen and also laid before Your Majesty a Confession. From it you may learn what the doctrine is that so inflames the rage of those madmen who today, with fire and sword, are troubling your kingdom. For I am not afraid to declare that what I have given here may be regarded as a summary of the very doctrine which, they loudly cry, ought to be punished with confiscation, exile, imprisonment, and flames, and wiped out by land and sea.

I am aware, indeed, how, to make our cause as hateful to Your Majesty as possible, they have filled your ears and mind with atrocious accusations. But you will be pleased, in your clemency, to consider that there could be no innocence in either word or deed if it were enough merely to accuse. When anyone, in order to stir up ill will, points out that this doctrine, of which I am trying to give Your Majesty an account, has been condemned by the votes of all the estates of the realm, and was long ago stabbed again and again by prejudiced sentences of the courts, he certainly says nothing more than this: that it has sometimes been violently crushed by the power and faction of its adversaries, and sometimes treacherously and deceitfully overwhelmed by lies, tricks, and slander. It is violence to pass bloody sentences against a cause while it is unheard; it is fraud to charge it, against what it deserves, with sedition and wrongdoing.

That no one may think we are unjust in complaining like this, you yourself, most illustrious Sovereign, can bear us witness with what lying slanders it is maligned in your presence every day: that it aims at nothing else than to wrench the scepters of kings out of their hands, to overturn all courts and seats of justice, to subvert all order and government, to disturb the peace and quiet of the people, to abolish all laws, to destroy the distinctions of rank and property, and, in short, to turn everything upside down. And yet what you hear is only the smallest part of what is said. For certain horrible charges are spread among the common people, charges which, if they were true, would justify the whole world in condemning the doctrine with its authors to a thousand fires and gallows. Who can wonder that public hatred is inflamed against it, when those most unjust accusations are believed? See why all ranks unite with one accord in condemning our persons and our doctrine!

Carried away by this feeling, those who sit in judgment merely pronounce as sentences the prejudices they have brought from home. They think they have properly done their part if they do not order anyone to be punished until he is convicted, either by his own confession or by legal evidence. But convicted of what crime? “Of that condemned doctrine,” is the answer. But with what justice was it condemned? The very heart of the defense was not to renounce the doctrine itself, but to maintain its truth. On this subject, however, not a whisper is allowed!

Justice, then, most invincible Sovereign, entitles me to ask that you undertake a full investigation of this cause, which until now has been tossed about anyhow and handled in the most irregular way, without any order of law, and with passionate heat rather than judicial gravity.

Let no one imagine that I am here composing my own private defense, in order to secure a safe return to my native land. Though I have toward it the feelings that become me as a man, still, as things now stand, I can be away from it without regret. The cause I plead is the common cause of all the godly, and therefore the very cause of Christ. Throughout your realm it now lies, as it were, in despair, torn and trampled on in every kind of way, and that more through the tyranny of certain Pharisees than by any sanction from yourself. But there is no point in asking here how this happens; that it happens cannot be denied. For the wicked have prevailed so far that the truth of Christ, if not utterly routed and scattered, lies hidden as if ignobly buried, while the poor Church, either wasted by cruel slaughter, or driven into exile, or intimidated and terror-struck, scarcely dares to breathe. And even now her enemies press on with their usual rage and fury over the ruins they have made, vigorously assaulting the wall, which is already giving way. Meanwhile, no man comes forward to offer his protection against such furies. Those who would like to be thought most favorable to the truth merely talk of pardoning the error and imprudence of ignorant men. For so those modest men#footnote[“Modesti homines,” not in Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] speak, giving the name of #emph[error and imprudence] to what they know to be#footnote[“Quam norunt,” not in Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] the infallible truth of God, and of #emph[ignorant men] to those whose intellect they see that Christ has not despised, since he has deigned to entrust them with the mysteries of his heavenly wisdom.#footnote[The words, “Quorum ingenium non adeo despicabile Christo fuisse vident,” not in Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] So all are ashamed of the Gospel.

Your duty, most serene Prince, is not to shut either your ears or your mind against so just a defense, especially in a cause involving such mighty interests as these: how the glory of God is to be kept unharmed on the earth, how the truth of God is to keep its dignity, how the kingdom of Christ is to continue among us whole and secure. The cause is worthy of your ear, worthy of your investigation, worthy of your throne.

The mark of a true sovereign is to acknowledge that, in the administration of his kingdom, he is a minister of God. He who does not make his reign serve the divine glory acts the part not of a king, but of a robber. Moreover, he deceives himself who expects long prosperity for any kingdom that is not ruled by the scepter of God, that is, by his divine word. For the heavenly oracle cannot fail which has declared that “where there is no vision, the people perish” (Prov 29:18).

Do not let a contemptuous idea of our lowliness turn you away from investigating this cause. We, indeed, are fully aware how poor and abject we are: in the presence of God we are miserable sinners, and in the sight of men most despised. We are (if you like) the mere dregs and refuse of the world, or worse, if anything worse can be named. So before God nothing remains for us to glory in except his mercy alone, by which, without any merit of our own, we have been admitted to the hope of eternal salvation;#footnote[The words stand thus in the Ed. 1536: “Qua salvi nullo nostro merito facti sumus.” (Beveridge’s note.)] and before men not even this much remains,#footnote[“Non ita multum,” not in Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] since we can glory only in our weakness, a thing which, in the eyes of men, it is the greatest disgrace even silently#footnote[“Cum nutu,” not in Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] to confess. But our doctrine must stand exalted above all the glory of the world, and unconquerable by all its power, because it is not ours, but that of the living God and his Anointed. The Father has appointed him King, that he may rule from sea to sea, and from the rivers to the ends of the earth; and so rule as to strike the whole earth, with its strength of iron and bronze and its splendor of gold and silver, with the mere rod of his mouth, and break them in pieces like a potter’s vessel, according to the magnificent predictions of the prophets concerning his kingdom (Dan 2:34; Isa 11:4; Ps 2:9).

Our adversaries, indeed, loudly maintain that our appeal to the word of God is a mere pretext, and that we are in fact its worst corrupters. How far this is not only malicious slander but also shameless effrontery, you will be able to judge for yourself by reading our Confession. Here, however, it may be necessary to make some remarks which may move you, or at least help you, to read and study it with attention.

When Paul declared that all prophecy ought to be according to the analogy of faith (Rom 12:6), he laid down the surest rule for testing the interpretation of Scripture. Let our doctrine be tested by this rule, and our victory is secure. For what agrees better and more aptly with faith than to acknowledge ourselves stripped of all strength so that we may be clothed by God, empty of all goodness so that we may be filled by Him, the slaves of sin so that he may set us free, blind so that he may enlighten us, lame so that he may guide us, and feeble so that he may sustain us; to strip ourselves of all ground of glorying so that he alone may shine forth glorious, and we may be glorified in Him? When we say these things, and others like them, they interrupt and complain that in this way we overturn some blind light of nature, imagined preparations, free will, and works meritorious of eternal salvation, with their supererogations as well;#footnote[The only word in the Ed. 1536 after “free will,” is “merita.” (Beveridge’s note.)] because they cannot bear that the entire praise and glory of all goodness, virtue, justice, and wisdom should remain with God. But we do not read of anyone being blamed for drinking too much from the fountain of living water; on the contrary, those are severely rebuked who “have dug their own cisterns—broken cisterns that cannot hold water” (Jer 2:13). Again, what is more agreeable to faith than to feel assured that God is a gracious Father when Christ is acknowledged as a brother and propitiator? Or than confidently to expect all prosperity and gladness from Him whose unspeakable love toward us was such that He “did not spare His own Son but gave Him up for us all” (Rom 8:32)? Or than to rest in the sure hope of salvation and eternal life whenever we think of Christ, in whom such treasures are hidden, as given by the Father? Here they attack us, and loudly maintain that this sure confidence is not free from arrogance and presumption. But as nothing is to be presumed of ourselves, so all things are to be presumed of God; and we are stripped of vainglory for no other reason than that we may learn to glory in the Lord. Why go further? Look over, most valiant King, all the parts of our cause, and count us the most wicked of all wicked men, if you do not find plainly that “to this end we labor and suffer reproach, because we have set our hope on the living God” (1 Tim 4:10); because we believe it to be “eternal life” to know “the only true God, and Jesus Christ,” whom he has sent (John 17:3). For this hope some of us are in chains, some beaten with rods, some made a public spectacle, some outlawed, some most cruelly tortured, some forced to flee; we are all pressed by hardships, loaded with dreadful curses, torn by slanders, and treated with the greatest indignity.

Look now at our adversaries (I mean the priesthood, at whose nod and pleasure others carry on their hostility against us), and consider with me for a little what zeal drives them. The true religion that is handed down in the Scriptures, and that all ought to hold, they readily allow both themselves and others to be ignorant of, to neglect and despise. They think it matters little what each man believes or does not believe about God and Christ, provided he submits to the judgment of the Church with what they call#footnote[“Ut aiunt,” not in Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] implicit faith. Nor are they much troubled if they see the glory of God dishonored by open blasphemies, provided no one raises a finger against the primacy of the Apostolic See and the authority of holy mother Church.#footnote[No part of this sentence from “provided” is in the Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] Why, then, do they fight for the mass, purgatory, pilgrimage, and similar trifles with such fierceness and bitterness that, though they cannot prove one of them from the word of God, they deny that piety can be safe without faith in these things—the most explicit faith, if I may put it that way? Why? Simply because their belly is their God, and their kitchen their religion; and they believe that if these were taken away, they would be not only not Christians, but not even men. For although some wallow in luxury and others live on thin crusts, they all live from the same pot, which without that fuel would not only cool but freeze solid. So the man who is most anxious about his stomach proves the fiercest champion of his faith. In short, the aim on which all of them to a man are bent is to keep their kingdom safe or their belly full; not one gives even the smallest sign of sincere zeal.

Nevertheless, they do not cease to attack our doctrine, and to accuse and defame it in whatever terms they can, in order to make it either hated or suspected. They call it new, and of recent birth; they carp at it as doubtful and uncertain; they ask us to say by what miracles it has been confirmed; they ask whether it is fair to accept it against the consensus of so many holy Fathers and the most ancient custom; they press us to confess either that it is schismatic in waging war on the Church, or that the Church must have been lifeless during the many centuries in which nothing of the kind was heard. Lastly, they say there is little need of argument, for its quality may be known by its fruits, namely, the large number of sects, the many seditious disturbances, and the great licentiousness which it has produced. No doubt it is a very easy matter for them to insult an undefended cause before an ignorant and credulous multitude; but if we were given a chance to speak in turn, the bitterness that they now pour out on us in foaming torrents, with as much license as impunity,#footnote[“Tam licenter quam impune,” not in Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] would certainly boil dry.

1\. First, in calling it new, they do great injury to God, whose sacred word did not deserve to be charged with novelty. To them, indeed, I have very little doubt it is new, as Christ is new and the Gospel is new; but those who know the old saying of Paul, that Christ Jesus “died for our sins, and rose again for our justification” (Rom 4:25), will find nothing new in us. That it long lay buried and unknown is the guilty result of man’s impiety; but now that it is restored to us by the kindness of God, it ought to resume its antiquity, just as a returning citizen resumes his rights.

2\. It is owing to the same ignorance that they hold it to be doubtful and uncertain; for this is the very thing the Lord complains of through his prophet: “The ox knows its owner, and the donkey its master’s manger, but Israel does not know; My people do not understand” (Isa 1:3). But however they may make sport of its uncertainty, if they had to seal their own doctrine with their blood and at the cost of their lives, it would be seen what value they put on it. Our confidence is very different—a confidence that is not daunted by the terrors of death, nor even by the judgment-seat of God itself.

3\. In demanding miracles from us, they act dishonestly. For we have not coined some new gospel, but keep the very one whose truth is confirmed by all the miracles that Christ and the apostles ever performed. But they have something special that we lack—they can confirm their faith by constant miracles down to the present day! On the contrary, they put forward miracles that could unsettle minds otherwise well disposed, so frivolous and ridiculous are they, so empty and false. But even if they were exceedingly wonderful, they could have no force against the truth of God, whose name ought to be hallowed always and everywhere, whether by miracles or by the natural course of events. The deception would perhaps be more plausible if Scripture did not teach us the lawful end and use of miracles. Mark tells us (Mark 16:20) that the signs which followed the preaching of the apostles were performed to confirm it; so Luke also relates that the Lord “affirmed the message of His grace” by granting signs and wonders to be done by the hands of the apostles (Acts 14:3). Very much to the same effect are the words of the apostle, that the salvation proclaimed in the gospel was confirmed, “the Lord bearing witness with signs and wonders, and with various miracles” (Heb 2:4). Shall we turn the things we are told are the seals of the gospel to the overthrow of the gospel? Shall we misuse what was meant only to confirm the truth for the confirming of lies? The proper course, therefore, is first to examine and test the doctrine which, the Evangelist says, comes first; then, once it has been proved, but not before, it may receive confirmation from miracles. But the mark of sound doctrine given by our Savior himself is that it tends to promote the glory not of men, but of God (John 7:18; 8:50). Since our Savior has declared this to be the test of doctrine, we are mistaken if we regard as miracles works that are used for any purpose other than to magnify the name of God.#footnote[No part of the passage, beginning above, “The deception,” &c., is in Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] And we should remember that Satan has his miracles, which, although they are tricks rather than true wonders, are still such as to delude the ignorant and unwary. Magicians and enchanters have always been famous for miracles, and astonishing miracles have nourished idolatry; yet these do not win us over to the superstitions either of magicians or of idolaters. In old times, too, the Donatists used their power of working miracles as a battering ram, with which they shook the simplicity of the common people. We now give our opponents the answer which Augustine then gave the Donatists (in Joan. Tract. 13): “The Lord put us on our guard against those wonder-workers when he foretold that false prophets would arise who, by lying signs and various wonders, would deceive even the elect, if that were possible” (Matt 24:24). Paul, too, gave warning that the reign of antichrist would come “with every kind of power, sign, and false wonder” (2 Thess 2:9).

But our opponents tell us that their miracles are performed not by idols, not by sorcerers, not by false prophets, but by saints: as if we did not know that it is one of Satan’s wiles to disguise himself as “an angel of light” (2 Cor 11:14). The Egyptians, among whom Jeremiah was buried, in ancient times offered sacrifices and paid other divine honors to him (Hieron. in Praef. Jerem). Did they not abuse the holy prophet of God for idolatry? And yet, as a reward for venerating his tomb in this way, they thought#footnote[Instead of “thought they were cured,” the Ed. 1536 says simply, “they were cured” (curarentur). (Beveridge’s note.)] that they were cured of the bite of serpents. What, then, shall we say but that it has been, and always will be, a most just punishment of God to send on those who do not receive the truth with love for it “a powerful delusion so that they believe the lie” (2 Thess 2:11)? We, then, have no lack of miracles, sure miracles that cannot be disputed; but those our opponents lay claim to are sheer delusions of Satan, since they draw the people away from the true worship of God to futility.

4\. They slanderously set the Fathers against us (I mean the ancient writers of a purer age), as if the Fathers were supporters of their impiety. If the contest were to be decided by such authority (to speak in the most moderate terms), the better part of the victory would be ours.#footnote[“Ut modestissime etiam loquar,” not in the Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] There is much that is admirable and wise in the writings of those Fathers, while in some things it has gone with them as with ordinary men. Yet these pious sons, of course, with the special sharpness of intellect, and judgment, and soul that belongs to them, adore only their slips and errors, while the things that are well said they either overlook, or disguise, or corrupt; so that one may truly say their only care has been to gather dross among gold. Then, with dishonest clamor, they attack us as enemies and despisers of the Fathers. So far are we from despising them that, if this were the proper place, it would give us no trouble to support most of the doctrines we now hold by their testimony. Yet in studying their writings we have tried to remember (1 Cor 3:21–23)#footnote[See also Augustin. Ep. 28. (Beveridge’s note.)], that all things are ours, to serve us, not to lord it over us, but that we belong to Christ alone, and must obey him in all things without exception. He who does not draw this distinction will have no fixed principles in religion; for those holy men were ignorant of many things, often contradict one another, and are sometimes at odds with themselves.

It is not without cause (our opponents remark) that we are warned by Solomon, “Do not move the ancient landmarks that your fathers have set” (Prov 22:28). But the same rule does not apply to the measuring of fields and to the obedience of faith. The rule for the latter is, “Forget your people and your father’s house” (Ps 45:10). But if they are so fond of allegory, why do they not take the apostles, rather than any other class of Fathers, to be the ones whose landmarks it is unlawful to remove? This is the interpretation of Jerome, whose words they have quoted in their canons. But as for those to whom they apply the passage, if they want the landmarks to be fixed, why do they themselves leap over them so freely whenever it suits their purpose?

Among the Fathers there were two, one of whom said,#footnote[i. Acatius in lib. 11 cap 16, F. Tripart. Hist.] “Our God neither eats nor drinks, and therefore has no need of chalices and plates”; and the other#footnote[ii. Ambr. lib. 2. De Officiis, cap. 28.] “Sacred rites do not require gold, and things that are not bought with gold do not please by gold.” They step beyond the boundary, therefore, when in sacred things they take such delight in gold, silver, ivory, marble, gems, and silks that, unless everything is overlaid with costly show, or rather insane luxury#footnote[Instead of the words here translated—namely “exquisito splendore vel potius insano luxu,” the Ed. 1536 has only the word “luxu.” (Beveridge’s note.)], they think God is not properly worshiped.

It was a Father who said,#footnote[iii. Spiridion. Trip. Hist. lib. 1 cap. 10.] “He ate meat freely on the day on which others abstained from it, because he was a Christian.” They leap over the boundaries, therefore, when they doom to perdition every soul that has tasted meat during Lent.

There were two Fathers, one of whom said,#footnote[iv. Trip. Hist. lib. 8 cap 1] “A monk who does not labor with his own hands is no better than a violent man and a robber”; and the other,#footnote[August. De Opere Monach cap 17] “Monks, however diligent they may be in study, meditation, and prayer, must not live off others.” This boundary, too, they crossed when they put lazy, gluttonous monks in dens and brothels, to gorge themselves on other men’s goods.

It was a Father who said,#footnote[vi. Epiph. Epist. ab Hieron. versa] “It is a horrible abomination to see in Christian churches a painted image either of Christ or of any saint.” Nor was this pronounced by the voice of a single individual; an Ecclesiastical Council also decreed,#footnote[vii. Conc. Elibert. can. 36.] “Let nothing that is worshiped be painted on walls.”#footnote[No part of this sentence is in Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] They are very far from keeping within these boundaries when they leave not a corner without images.

Another Father advised,#footnote[viii. Ambr de Abraha. lib. i c. 7] “That after performing the duty of kindness to the dead in their burial, we should leave them at rest.” They burst through these limits when they keep up a constant anxiety about the dead.

It is a Father who testifies,#footnote[ix. Gelasius Papa in Conc. Rom.] “That the substance of bread and wine in the Eucharist does not cease but remains, just as the nature and substance of man remains united to the Godhead in the Lord Jesus Christ.” They pass this boundary when they claim that, as soon as the words of our Lord are pronounced, the substance of bread and wine ceases, and is transubstantiated into body and blood.

They were Fathers who, as they offered only one Eucharist to the whole Church,#footnote[x. Chrys. in 1. cap. Ephes.] and kept back from it the profane and the shameless, so in the severest terms condemned all those#footnote[xi. Calixt. Papa, De Consecrat. dist. 2] who were present but did not take communion. How far they have moved these landmarks, filling not churches only but also private houses with their masses, admitting anyone at all to be present, each the more willingly the more he pays, however wicked and impure he may be—inviting no one to faith in Christ and faithful communion in the sacraments, but rather selling their own work in place of the grace and merits of Christ!#footnote[Instead of the whole passage beginning “It is a Father who testifies,” &c., the Ed. 1536 has the following sentence: “Ex patribus erat qui negavit in sacramento coenae esse verum corpus sed mysterium duntaxat corporis; sic enim ad verbum loquitur.” On the margin, reference is made to the author of an unfinished Tract on Matthew, forming the 11th Homil. among the works of Chrysostom. (Beveridge’s note.)]

There were two Fathers, one of whom decided that those were to be wholly excluded from partaking of Christ’s sacred supper#footnote[xii. Gelas. can. Comperimus, De Consec. dist. 2.] who, content with communion in one kind, abstained from the other; while the other Father strongly contends#footnote[xiii. Cypr. Epist. 2, lib. 1. De Lapsis.] that the blood of the Lord ought not to be denied to the Christian people, who, in confessing him, are commanded to shed their own blood. These landmarks, too, they removed when, by an unalterable law, they ordered the very thing which the former Father punished with excommunication, and the latter condemned for a valid reason.

It was a Father who declared it rashness,#footnote[xiv. August. lib. 2 De Peccat. Mer. cap. ult.] in an obscure question, to decide either way without clear and evident authority from Scripture. They forgot this landmark when they enacted so many constitutions, so many canons, and so many dogmatic decisions without sanction from the word of God.

It was a Father who rebuked Montanus, among other heresies,#footnote[xv. Apollon. De quo Eccles. Hist. lib 5 cap. 12.] for being the first to impose laws of fasting. They have gone far beyond this landmark too in imposing fasting under the strictest laws.

It was a Father who denied#footnote[xvi. Paphnut. Tripart. Hist. lib. 2 cap. 14.] that the ministers of the Church should be forbidden to marry, and declared married life to be a state of chastity; and there were other Fathers who agreed with his decision. They overstepped these boundaries in rigidly binding their priests to celibacy.

It was a Father who held#footnote[xvii. Cypr. Epist. 2, lib. 2] that Christ alone should be listened to, because it is said, “Listen to Him!”; and that we should regard not what others before us have said or done, but only what Christ, the head of all, has commanded. This landmark they neither observe themselves nor allow others to observe, while they set over themselves and others any master whatever rather than Christ.

There is a Father who contends#footnote[xviii. Aug. cap. 2, Cont. Cresconium Grammat.] that the Church ought not to put herself before Christ, because he always judges truly, whereas church judges, being but men, are generally deceived. Having burst through this barrier too, they do not hesitate to make the whole authority of Scripture hang on the judgment of the Church.#footnote[No part of this passage is in Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)]

All the Fathers with one heart abhorred, and with one mouth protested#footnote[xix. Calv. De Scholast. Doctor. Judicium. See Book II. cap. 2 sec. 6; Book III. cap. 4 sec. 1, 2, 7, 13, 14, 26-29; Book III. cap. 11 sec. 14, 15; Book IV. cap. 18 sec. 1; and cap. 19 sec. 10, 11, 22, 23. (Beveridge’s note.)] against, polluting the word of God with the subtleties of sophists and entangling it in the brawls of logicians. Do they keep within these limits when the sole occupation of their lives is to wrap up and entangle the simplicity of Scripture in endless disputes and worse than sophistical jargon? So much so that, if the Fathers were to rise from their graves and listen to the brawling art that bears the name of speculative theology, there is nothing they would less suppose it to be than a discussion about God.

But my discourse would far exceed its proper limits if I were to show in detail how insolently those men shake off the yoke of the Fathers, while they wish to be thought their most obedient sons. Months, even years, would not be enough for me; and yet their effrontery is so deplorable and desperate that they presume to rebuke us for overstepping the ancient landmarks!

5\. Then, again, it is useless for them to call us to the bar of custom. To make everything yield to custom would be to do the greatest injustice. If the judgments of mankind were sound, custom would be set by the good. But in fact it is often far otherwise; for whatever the many are seen to do at once gains the force of custom. But human affairs have hardly ever been so happily ordered that the better course pleased the greater number. So the private vices of the multitude have generally resulted in public error, or rather in that common consent in vice which these worthy men would have to be law. Anyone with eyes may see that it is not one flood of evils that has deluged us; that many deadly plagues have invaded the globe; that everything is rushing headlong; so that either the affairs of men must be altogether despaired of, or we must not only resist but boldly attack the prevailing evils. The cure is kept away for no other reason than the length of time we have been accustomed to the disease. But granted that public error must have a place in human society, still in the kingdom of God we must look and listen only to his eternal truth, against which no length of years, no custom, no conspiracy can claim a right by long usage. Thus Isaiah long ago taught the elect of God: “Do not call conspiracy everything these people regard as conspiracy”—that is, do not join with the people in an impious consent—“Do not fear what they fear; do not live in dread. Sanctify the #smallcaps[Lord] of Hosts Himself; let Him be your#super[+] fear, and let Him be your#super[+] dread” (Isa 8:12). Now, therefore, let them, if they will, set against us both past ages and present examples; if we sanctify the Lord of hosts, we will not be greatly afraid. Though many ages should have agreed in the same ungodliness, He is strong who takes vengeance to the third and fourth generation; or though the whole world should league together in the same iniquity, He has shown by experience what the end is of those who sin with the multitude, when He destroyed the whole human race with a flood, saving Noah with his little family, who, standing alone in his faith, “condemned the world” (Heb 11:7). In short, depraved custom is just a kind of general plague, in which men perish none the less because they fall in a crowd. It would be well, moreover, to ponder the observation of Cyprian,#footnote[Epist. 3, lib. 2; et in Epist ad Julian. De Haeret. Baptiz.] that those who sin in ignorance, though they cannot be entirely cleared of blame, seem nevertheless to be in some sense excusable; whereas those who obstinately reject the truth, when it is offered to them by the kindness of God, have no defense to offer.#footnote[No part of this sentence is in Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)]

6\. Their dilemma does not press us so hard as to force us to confess either that the Church was lifeless for a considerable time, or that we are now in a quarrel with the Church. The Church of Christ has indeed lived, and will live, as long as Christ reigns at the right hand of the Father. By his hand it is sustained, by his protection defended, by his mighty power kept in safety. For he will certainly carry out what he once undertook: he will be with his people always, “even to the end of the age” (Matt 28:20). With the Church we wage no war, since with one consent, together with the whole body of the faithful, we worship and adore one God, and Christ Jesus the Lord, as all the godly have always adored him. But they themselves stray not a little from the truth in recognizing no church except the one they see with the bodily eye, and in trying to confine it within limits in which it cannot be contained.

The hinges on which the controversy turns are these: first, they contend that the form of the Church is always visible and apparent; and secondly, they place this form in the see of the Church of Rome and its hierarchy. We, on the contrary, maintain both that the Church may exist without any visible form, and also that its form is not known by that outward splendor which they foolishly marvel at, but by a very different mark, namely, by the pure preaching of the word of God and the proper administration of the sacraments. They raise an outcry whenever the Church cannot be pointed to with the finger. But how often was it the fate of the Church among the Jews to be so disfigured that no beauty appeared? What splendid form do we suppose it had when Elijah complained that he was left alone (1 Kgs 19:14)? How long after the coming of Christ did it lie hidden without form? How often since then has it been so oppressed by wars, seditions, and heresies that it was nowhere seen in splendor? Had they lived at that time, would they have believed there was any Church? But Elijah learned that there remained seven thousand men who had not bowed the knee to Baal; nor ought we to doubt that Christ has always reigned on earth ever since he ascended to heaven. Had the faithful at that time required some visible form, would they not at once have lost heart? And indeed Hilary counted it a very great fault in his day that men were so taken up with a foolish awe of the dignity of bishops that they did not see the deadly hydra lurking under that mask. His words are (Cont. Auxentium): “One warning I give: Beware of Antichrist; for, unhappily, a love of walls has seized you#super[+]\; unhappily, you#super[+] venerate the Church of God in houses and buildings; unhappily, under these you#super[+] bring in the name of peace. Is it doubtful that in these Antichrist will have his seat? Mountains, and woods, and lakes, and dungeons, and whirlpools are safer to me; for it was in these that the prophets prophesied, either dwelling in them or plunged into them.”

And what is it today that the world venerates in its horned bishops, except that it imagines those it sees presiding over famous cities to be holy prelates of religion? Away, then, with this absurd way of judging!#footnote[No part of the passage beginning above is in the Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] Let us rather reverently grant that, as God alone knows who are his (2 Tim 2:19), so he may sometimes withdraw the outward manifestation of his Church from the sight of men. This, I admit, is a fearful punishment which God sends on the earth; but if the wickedness of men deserves it, why do we strive to oppose the just vengeance of God?#footnote[In the last Ed., “justae Dei ultionis:” in Ed. 1536, “divinae justitiae.” (Beveridge’s note.)] It was in this way that God, in past ages, punished the ingratitude of men. For after they had refused to obey his truth, and had put out his light, he allowed them, blinded in their minds, both to be deluded by absurd lies and to be plunged in thick darkness, so that no face of a true Church appeared. Meanwhile, however, though his own people were scattered and hidden amid errors and darkness, he saved them from destruction. No wonder; for he knew how to preserve them even in the confusion of Babylon and the flame of the fiery furnace.

But as to the wish that the form of the Church should be judged by some kind of empty pomp, I will briefly point out rather than explain how dangerous it is, so as not to go beyond all bounds. What they say is that the Pontiff,#footnote[“Papa Romanus,” in the Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)] who holds the apostolic see, and the prelates who are anointed and consecrated by him,#footnote[Instead of the words, “qui ab eo antistites inuncti et consecrati, infulis modo et lituis insigniti sunt,” the Ed. 1536 has only “episcopi alii.” (Beveridge’s note.)] provided they bear the insignia of miters and crosiers, represent the Church, and ought to be considered as in the place of the Church, and therefore cannot err. Why so? Because they are pastors of the Church, and consecrated to the Lord. And were not Aaron and the other leaders of Israel pastors? Yet Aaron and his sons, though already set apart to the priesthood, erred all the same when they made the calf (Exod 32:4). Why, on this view, should not the four hundred prophets who lied to Ahab represent the Church (1 Kgs 22:8, &c.)? The Church, however, stood on the side of Micaiah. He was alone, indeed, and despised, but from his mouth the truth came forth. Did not the prophets also bear both the name and face of the Church when, with one accord, they rose up against Jeremiah, and boasted with threats that it was impossible that the law should perish from the priest, or counsel from the wise, or the word from the prophet (Jer 18:18)? Against the whole body of the prophets, Jeremiah is sent alone to declare from the Lord (Jer 4:9) that a time would come when the law would perish from the priest, counsel from the wise, and the word from the prophet. Was not the same splendor displayed in that council when the chief priests, scribes, and Pharisees assembled to consult how they might put Jesus to death (John 12:10)? Let them go, then, and cling to the outward mask, while they make Christ and all the prophets of God schismatics, and, on the other hand, make Satan’s ministers the instruments of the Holy Spirit!

But if they are sincere, let them answer me in good faith: in what place, and among whom, do they think the Church resided after the Council of Basle \[Basel\] degraded and deposed Eugenius from the papacy, and put Amadeus in his place? Try as they may, they cannot deny that that Council was legitimate as far as outward forms are concerned, and was summoned not by one Pontiff only, but by two. Eugenius, with the whole herd of cardinals and bishops who had joined him in plotting the dissolution of the Council, was there condemned for defiance, rebellion, and schism. Afterwards, however, helped by the favor of princes, he got his papacy back safe and sound. The election of Amadeus, duly made by the authority of a general holy synod, went up in smoke; only he himself was appeased with a cardinal’s hat, like a barking dog quieted with a scrap of meat. Out of the lap of these heretical, rebellious, and defiant men came all the popes, cardinals, bishops, abbots, and presbyters since. Here they are caught, and cannot escape. For to which party will they give the name of Church? Will they deny that it was a general Council, though it lacked nothing in outward majesty, having been solemnly summoned by two bulls, consecrated by the legate of the Roman See as its president, constituted properly in every respect, and keeping all its honors to the last? Will they admit that Eugenius and his whole train, through whom they have all been consecrated, were schismatics? Let them, then, either define the form of the Church differently, or, however many they are, we will hold them all to be schismatics for having knowingly and willingly received ordination from heretics. But even if it had never been found out before that the Church is not tied to outward pomp, they themselves furnish us with ample proof in their own conduct, proudly selling themselves to the world under the plausible title of Church, though they are the deadly plagues of the Church. I do not speak of their morals and of the tragic atrocities with which their whole life teems, since they say that they are Pharisees who should be heard, not imitated. If you devote some part of your leisure to our writings, you will see clearly that their doctrine—the very doctrine to which they say they owe it that they are the Church—is a deadly murderer of souls, the firebrand, ruin, and destruction of the Church.

7\. Lastly, they are far from candid when they spitefully list the disturbances, tumults, and disputes which the preaching of our doctrine has brought in its train, and the fruits which it now produces in many cases; for the doctrine itself is undeservedly blamed for evils that ought to be laid to the malice of Satan. It is one of the marks of the divine word that whenever it appears, Satan stops slumbering and sleeping. This is the surest and most unfailing test for distinguishing it from false doctrines, which readily betray themselves by being received by all with willing ears and welcomed by an applauding world. So for some ages, during which all things were sunk in deep darkness, almost all mankind#footnote[For “cuncti fere mortales” the Ed. 1536 has only “homines.” (Beveridge’s note.)] were mere jest and sport to the god of this world, who, like some Sardanapalus, idled and lived in luxury undisturbed. For what else would he do but laugh and play while in quiet and undisputed possession of his kingdom? But when light shining from above somewhat scattered the darkness—when that Mighty One disturbed and struck his kingdom—then, indeed, he began to shake off his usual sluggishness and rush to arms. And first he stirred up the hands of men, so that by them he might violently suppress the dawning truth; but when this did him no good, he turned to snares, stirring up dissensions and disputes about doctrine by means of his Catabaptists and other monstrous scoundrels, so that he might in this way obscure, and at last extinguish, the truth. And now he persists in attacking it with both weapons, trying to pluck up the true seed by the violent hand of man, and striving, as far as he can, to choke it with his weeds, so that it may not grow and bear fruit. But it will be in vain if we listen to the warning of the Lord, who long ago revealed his wiles, so that we might not be taken unawares, and armed us with full protection against all his schemes. But how malicious it is to throw on the word of God itself the blame for the seditions that wicked men and rebels stir up against it, or for the sects that impostors raise against it! The example, however, is not new. Elijah was asked whether it was not he who troubled Israel. Christ was seditious, according to the Jews; and the apostles were charged with the crime of stirring up the people. What else are those doing who today blame us for all the disturbances, tumults, and contentions that break out against us? Elijah, however, has taught us our answer (1 Kgs 18:17, 18). It is not we who spread errors or stir up tumults, but they who resist the mighty power of God.

But while this single answer is enough to rebut the rash charges of these men, it is necessary, on the other hand, to provide for the weakness of those who are alarmed by such offenses and quite often waver in perplexity. So that they may not fall away in this perplexity and lose their standing, let them know that the apostles in their day experienced the very things that now happen to us. There were then unlearned and unstable men who, as Peter tells us (2 Pet 3:16), twisted the inspired writings of Paul to their own destruction. There were despisers of God who, when they heard that sin abounded in order that grace might abound all the more, at once inferred, “We will continue in sin so that grace may abound” (Rom 6:1); when they heard that believers were not under the law, but under grace, they promptly chimed in, “We will sin because we are not under the law, but under grace” (Rom 6:15). There were some who charged the apostle with being an encourager of evil. Many false apostles slipped in secretly to tear down the churches he had built up. Some preached the gospel out of envy and strife, not sincerely (Phil 1:15)—even maliciously—thinking to add affliction to his chains. Elsewhere the gospel made little progress. All sought their own interests, not the things of Jesus Christ. Others went back like the dog to its vomit, or the sow that was washed to its wallowing in the mire. Great numbers perverted their spiritual freedom into carnal license. False brethren crept in, to the grave danger of the faithful. Among the brethren themselves various quarrels arose. What, then, were the apostles to do? Were they either to keep quiet about it for a time, or rather to lay aside and abandon that gospel which they saw to be the seedbed of so many quarrels, the source of so many dangers, the occasion of so many offenses? In straits of this kind they remembered that Christ was “a stone of stumbling and a rock of offense,” “appointed to cause the fall and rise of many,” and “to be a sign that will be spoken against” (Luke 2:34); and, armed with this assurance, they went on boldly through all the dangers of tumults and offenses. We should be upheld by the same thought, since Paul declares that it is an unfailing characteristic of the gospel to be “an aroma of death to death among those who are perishing” (2 Cor 2:16), although it was rather meant for us to be an aroma of life to life, and the power of God for the salvation of believers. This we would certainly find it to be, if we did not by our ingratitude corrupt this unspeakable gift of God, and turn to our destruction what ought to be our only saving defense.#footnote[Instead of the concluding part of the sentence beginning “though rather,” &c., and stopping at the reference, the Ed. 1536 simply continues the quotation “odor vitae in vitam iis qui salvi sunt.” (Beveridge’s note.)]

But to return, Sire.#footnote[Instead of “Rex” simply, the Ed. 1536 has “magnanime Rex.” (Beveridge’s note.)] Do not be moved by the absurd charges with which our adversaries are trying to frighten you into believing that nothing else is sought and aimed at by this new gospel (for so they call it) than an opportunity for sedition and impunity for every kind of vice. Our God#footnote[Instead of “Deus noster,” the Ed. 1536 has only “Deus.” (Beveridge’s note.)] is not the author of division, but of peace; and the Son of God, who came to destroy the works of the devil, is not the minister of sin. We, too, are undeservedly charged with desires of a kind for which we have never given even the smallest ground for suspicion. We, of course, are plotting the overthrow of kingdoms—we, whose voice was never heard in faction, and whose life, while lived under you, is known to have been always quiet and simple; who even now, exiled from our home, still do not cease to pray for every blessing on your person and your kingdom! We, of course, are seeking unchecked indulgence in vice—we, in whose conduct, though there is much to be blamed, there is nothing that deserves such a charge; nor (thank God) have we profited so little in the gospel that our life cannot be, to these slanderers, an example of chastity, kindness, mercy, self-control, patience, moderation, or any other virtue. It is plain, indeed, that we fear God sincerely and worship him in truth, since, whether by life or by death, we desire his name to be hallowed; and hatred itself has been forced to bear witness to the innocence and civic integrity of some of our people who were put to death for the very thing that deserved the highest praise. But if any, under pretext of the gospel, stir up tumults (none such have yet been found in your realm), if any use the liberty of the grace of God as a cloak for license (I know of many who do), there are laws and legal punishments by which they may be punished as they deserve—only, in the meantime, let not the gospel of God be spoken ill of because of the crimes of evil men.

Sire,#footnote[In Ed. 1536, “Rex magnificentissime” (Beveridge’s note.)] so that you may not lend too credulous an ear to the accusations of our enemies, their venomous injustice has been set before you at sufficient length; I fear even at more than sufficient length, since this preface has grown almost to the size of a full defense. My object, however, was not to compose a defense, but only, with a view to the hearing of our cause, to soften your mind, now indeed turned away and estranged from us—I add, even inflamed against us—but whose good will, we are confident, we would regain if you would only once read this our Confession calmly and quietly; we desire Your Majesty to accept it in place of a defense. But if the whispers of the malicious so possess your ear that the accused are to have no opportunity of pleading their cause; if those vindictive furies, with your connivance, are always to rage with chains, scourgings, tortures, mutilations, and burnings, we, indeed, like sheep doomed to slaughter, will be reduced to every extremity; yet in such a way that in our patience we will keep possession of our souls, and wait for the strong hand of the Lord, which will surely appear in its own time and show itself armed, both to rescue the poor from affliction and to take vengeance on the despisers who are now exulting in such complacency.#footnote[The words, “qui tanta securitate nunc exsultant,” not in Ed. 1536. (Beveridge’s note.)]

Most illustrious King, may the Lord, the King of kings, establish your throne in righteousness, and your scepter in equity.

#smallcaps[Basle], 1st #emph[August] 1536.
